<<<
tother, now only colloquial, was formerly in good literary use, & was then more often written tother than Vother; there is therefore no need for the apostrophe.
===
**tother,** now only colloquial, was formerly in good literary use, & was then more often written *tother* than *t'other*; there is therefore no need for the apostrophe.
>>>

<<<
toto caelo. Literally, ‘by the whole sky’, i.e. by the greatest

ossible distance. Properly used only with differ, different, & words of similar meaning;
===
**toto caelo.** Literally, 'by the whole sky', i.e. by the greatest possible distance. Properly used only with *differ, different,* & words of similar meaning;
>>>

<<<
guessed that it is a high-class variant of totally: ... had the effect of habitually repealing its own canon in part, during the life-time of parties ..., & of repealing it, toto caelo, afier the death of etther of them. See FOREIGN DANGER.
===
guessed that it is a high-class variant of *totally*: *. . . had the effect of habitually repealing its own canon* in part, *during the life-time of parties . . ., & of repealing* it, toto caelo, *after the death of either of them.* See ^^FOREIGN DANGER^^.
>>>

